/*! \page developer_backend_rp_page Rp Namespace

\ref developer_backend_field_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_test_page         (Next)
<BR>

The Rp namespace contains a set of class templates that are used to
construct the pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg executables. Every class template
defined in this namespaces takes the dimension of space, D, and a 
backend parameter, T, as template parameters.  The pscf_rpc executable 
is constructed using a set of specializations of these templates that
all have a backend template argument T=CPT, while pscf_rpg is built 
using specializations with T=CUT.

Each of executable programs constructed by the PSCF package is 
designed using a hierarchical system of object ownership. Almost every 
object used in a PSCF program is a "child" that is owned by an instance 
of some higher-level "parent" class.  In this system, each "child" object 
is either a member variable of its parent, or is a dynamically allocated 
object that is accessed through a pointer that is a member of the parent 
object, which the parent is responsible for creating and destroying the 
child.  The top of this ownership hierarchy for pscf_rpc and pscf_rpc is 
a class template named System \<D,T\> that directly or indirectly owns 
every object used by the program.  

In the Rp namespace, the same arguments for the parameters D and T 
are used through objects in this ownership hierarchy: 
An object that is an instance of a class template specialization with 
a specific pair of arguments for D and T may be owned by a parent 
that is an instance of another class template specialization with the 
same two values for these two parameter, and may own children that are 
instantiated using the same two template arguments.  An instance of 
System \<D,T\> with a specific choice of values for these template 
parameters (e.g., D=3 and T=CUT) thus directly or indirectly a set of 
other objects that are instances of class template specializations that 
use the same two arguments for these parameters.  Specifically, such a 
System \<D,T\> object indirectly owns a set of RField \<D,T\> defined 
using the same values of D and T, and is thus designed for the same
dimension of space and the same backend.

When PSCF is compiled, instantiation of template specializations in
the Rp namespaces that use a particular backend is controlled by a 
system of makefile variables and associated preprocessor macros:
GPU-enabled specializations with T=CUT are instantiated (i.e., compiled) 
for  possible values of D=1,2 and 3 if and only if the preprocessor 
macro PSCF_CUDA is defined.  Specializations with T=CPT and D=1,2, 
and 3 are instantiated if and only if the macro PSCF_CPP is defined.
If both backends are enabled, 6 specializations of each class
template in namespace Rp are thus compiled when the package is built.

<BR>
